    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to the establishment and\npowers and duties of the council on children and families; to repeal certain\nprovisions of such law relating thereto; and to repeal certain provisions of\nthe mental hygiene law relating to the children's plan",
    "basePrintNo": "S9004",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9004-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§483, 483-a, 483-b & 483-g, rpld §§483-c - 483-f, add §483-c, Soc Serv L; rpld §7.43, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9004\n\nSPONSOR: BRISPORT\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the social services law, in relation to the establish-\nment and powers and duties of the council on children and families; to\nrepeal certain provisions of such law relating thereto; and to repeal\ncertain provisions of the mental hygiene law relating to the children's\nplan\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to update and strengthen the coordinating\nrole of the New York State Council on Children and Families (CCF).\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 would amend Social Services Law (SSL) § 483 by adding a\npurpose and making technical corrections including updating CCF member\nagency names that have changed in recent years and amending the title of\nthe head of CCF to reflect the current title for this position.\n\nSection 2 would amend SSL § 483-a by removing the reference to the chair\nof CCF and clarifying that the executive director of CCF may request\nagency assistance to carry out its powers and duties.\n\nSection 3 would amend SSL § 483-b by updating the powers of CCF to\nreflect current practices.\n\nSection 4 would replace the current SSL § 483-c, which sets forth the\nCoordinated Children's Services for Children with Emotional and/or\nBehavioral Disorders, with the creation of two standing committees to\ncarry out CCF's broad mandate to improve systems of care for children\nand youth: (1) the Cross-Systems Committee for Children and Youth with\nComplex Needs (to address the needs of youth who require assistance from\nmultiple systems) and (2) the Early Care and Education Committee (to\ncoordinate early childhood systems to promote healthy development and\nschool readiness). Both committees would be required to produce annual\nreports. This section also would allow for other committees to be formed\nto respond to evolving needs.\n\nSection 5 would repeal SSL §§ 483-d through 483-f and the corresponding\nchildren's plan provision in Mental Hygiene Law § 7.43.\n\nSection 6 would amend SSL § 483-g to specify that one of the two chair-\npersons designated by the governor for the Early Childhood Advisory\nCouncil (ECAC) would be the executive director of CCF or his or her\ndesignee.\n\nSection 7 would set forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis proposal updates and clarifies the comprehensive, cross-systems\nrole of CCF. Technical corrections include updating the names of CCF\nmember state agencies. Specifically, CCF is composed of the following\nmember agencies: the Office of Children and Family Services (OCFS), the\nOffice of Temporary and Disability Assistance (OTDA), the Office of\nMental Health (OMR), the Office for People with Developmental Disabili-\nties (OPWDD), the Office of Addiction Services and Supports (OASAS), the\nState Education Department (SED), the Department of Health (DOH), the\nDivision of Criminal Justice Services (DCJS), the Justice Center for the\nProtection of People with Special Needs (Justice Center), the State\nOffice for the Aging (SOFA) and the Department of Labor (DOL). In recent\nyears, several of the CCF member agency names have been updated, such as\nOASAS and OPWDD, and other agencies have been reconfigured, such as the\nJustice Center (formerly the \"Commission on Quality of Care for the\nMentally Disabled\") and the \"Office for Probation and Correctional\nAlternatives\" which had been a separate agency and now is a part of\nDCJS. This proposal also adds the Developmental Disabilities Planning\nCouncil (DDPC) to the group of CCF member state agencies, as the work of\nDDPC aligns and supports the work of CCF around system change and inter-\nagency capacity-building efforts that promote inclusion and self-deter-\nmination for persons with intellectual and developmental disabilities.\n\nIn addition, this proposal clarifies that CCF is led by an executive\ndirector (not a \"chief executive officer\") and eliminates the reference\nto CCF having a designated chair, which aligns with practice over the\npast several decades.\n\nThis proposal also creates two standing interagency committees under\nCCF. Together, the two committees will serve several purposes:  conduct-\ning the work charged to CCF in its establishing law and subsequent laws\nadding to its responsibilities, aligning CCF's work with current needs,\nand allowing for future flexibility for CCF to respond to emerging needs\nas identified by the governor, the executive director of CCF and the\ncommissioners and directors of member state agencies.\n\nThe first of the two standing committees that this bill creates is the\nCross-Systems Committee for Children and Youth with Complex Needs to\naddress the needs of children and youth who require services from multi-\nple systems. This will be a commissioner-level committee to be chaired\nby the executive director of CCF. This committee will unify existing\nefforts and create a single point of accountability under the leadership\nof CCF.\n\nSeveral existing CCF initiatives that are part of current law would be\namended to reflect this committee. The work of the Coordinated Chil-\ndren's Services Initiative and the Children's Plan requirements would be\ncarried out through this committee, as would the work of the Out-of-\nState Placement Committee. The Outof-State Placement Committee was\ncreated to reduce the number of children and youth from New York being\nplaced in out-of-state residential schools and programs. Since its\ncreation, out-of-state placements have dramatically decreased.1 To\nensure that out-of-state placements continue to be reduced, this legis-\nlative proposal would have CCF, through its Cross-Systems Committee for\nChildren and Youth with Complex Needs, continue to monitor and report on\nout-of-state placements. The Restraint and Crisis Intervention Technique\nCommittee in CCF statute also would be eliminated. However, if needed,\nthe Cross-Systems Committee could be expressly charged with focusing on\nthe issue of the use of restraints and seclusion in child-serving\nprograms. Notably, DDPC now is leading an interagency effort, with CCF\nbeing an active member, to further reduce the use of restraints and\nseclusion.\n\nThe second of the two standing committees that this bill creates is the\nEarly Care and Education Committee. Although CCF's mission and work\ninvolve children of all ages, with a growing body of research in recent\nyears highlighting the importance of the early years in laying the foun-\ndation for lifelong learning, behavior and health, this standing commit-\ntee strengthens CCF's support for young children and responds to early\ncare and education experts who have been advocating for years for New\nYork State to create a single office for early childhood. Accordingly,\nCCF would formally be responsible for leading the interagency Early Care\nand Education Committee, which would meet regularly to address and\nresolve any cross-system issues and barriers to serving young children.\nAs the New York State Final Report of the Child Care Availability Task\nForce stated, this role would build on the existing interagency and\ncoordinating role of CCF. Currently, the Early Childhood Advisory Coun-\ncil is codified under CCF and the following initiatives are housed at\nCCF: the federal Preschool Development Birth through Five grant, the New\nYork State Head Start Collaboration Project, the federal Early Childhood\nComprehensive Systems: Prenatal to Three program and the Pyramid Model\nfor Supporting the Social and Emotional Development of Infants and Young\nChildren.\n\nSince CCF was codified in 1977, it has worked to promote the success of\nNew York's children and families by facilitating collaboration among the\nstate's child-serving agencies. This collective approach to helping\nchildren reach their full potential recognizes that children and fami-\nlies touch multiple systems. With the implementation of these amendments\nto the CCF statute, New York's child-serving delivery system would be\nstronger and more effective so that every child and family have the\nopportunity to thrive.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nDepartmental  120 (2021).\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred and eightieth day after it\nshall become a law.\n",
